    "slot_key": "Rights metadata used to determine access",
    "slot_value": "Access may vary according to rights; public and NIH access to approved content, with changing access rules over time, and access must be role-based and sometimes item-specific, with controls over what can be viewed, downloaded, or edited by each type of user. Rights and compliance should be the deciding factor when access, preservation, ingest speed, or collection-specific needs point in different directions. Staff should follow the standard process unless there is a documented, approved exception that applies to that specific collection or item. The exception should be tied to a named authority, be current, and be visible in the record so staff can justify why they did not use the default rule. If there’s any doubt, the default should win until a manager or rights authority confirms the exception. Anonymous users should be able to search and view only materials explicitly permitted for public access, and anything not cleared for public viewing should be blocked at the access control step. During normal load, public search and viewing should continue normally; when performance degrades, public discovery should remain available but may be slowed or queued before anything that affects staff administration, and already permitted public materials should not be blocked unless there is no other option. Staff should be able to review the original preserved bitstream, edit the placeholder or restricted metadata, update the rights decision when it changes, and trigger release or make the item public once the review is complete and the object is cleared for access. These actions should be tracked for auditability, showing who changed what and when.",
